x509: trust a self-signed anchor whose keyUsage lacks keyCertSign

Path building matched candidate issuers with X509_check_issued, which
rejects any issuer whose keyUsage extension lacks keyCertSign. A
self-signed end-entity certificate (keyUsage = digitalSignature,
keyEncipherment, the shape of `dotnet dev-certs` and `openssl req -x509`
server certificates) therefore never matched the copy of itself in the
trust store, and did not get EXFLAG_SS either. Verification failed with
X509_V_ERR_UNABLE_TO_GET_ISSUER_CERT_LOCALLY even though the operator
pinned that exact certificate. OpenSSL 3 trusts it.

Split X509_check_issued the way OpenSSL 3 does:

- x509_likely_issued: subject/issuer name and authority key identifier.
  Path building (x509_check_issued_with_callback, and so find_issuer,
  X509_STORE_CTX_get1_issuer and the self-signed checks) uses this.
- x509_signing_allowed: the keyCertSign check. internal_verify applies
  it on every issuer edge of the final chain (RFC 5280, 6.1.4 (n)) and
  reports X509_V_ERR_KEYUSAGE_NO_CERTSIGN with the issuer as the current
  certificate. A self-signed certificate is its own issuer, not an edge,
  so it is exempt, as in OpenSSL 3.
- EXFLAG_SS no longer requires keyCertSign.
- X509_check_issued keeps both checks for external callers.

Visible changes: a pinned self-signed certificate without keyCertSign
verifies. The unpinned one reports DEPTH_ZERO_SELF_SIGNED_CERT instead
of UNABLE_TO_GET_ISSUER_CERT_LOCALLY. An untrusted intermediate without
keyCertSign now reaches check_chain_extensions and fails with
INVALID_CA. A trusted issuer without keyCertSign fails with
KEYUSAGE_NO_CERTSIGN. All three match OpenSSL 3.

Upstream: oven-sh/boringssl, on top of 41bf9b59c2ebf277a7aa427e1ecad5cc80dd4d4f.

